# Example `<repo_root>/crustify/.gitignore`.
#
# Two jobs. It keeps DERIVED artifacts out of git — they are regenerable, and
# excluding them keeps the `git worktree` snapshot down to the actual mutation
# surface (`rust/` source + the C edits). And it deliberately does NOT exclude
# the hand-authored inputs, which no rerun reproduces.
#
# Excluded does not mean hidden from agents: the derived trees are read-only
# across a wave, so `worktree.link_shared` symlinks the single shared copy into
# each agent worktree rather than duplicating it.
#
# No trailing slashes on the excluded dirs: they are real directories in the
# main checkout but appear as SYMLINKS inside per-agent worktrees, and a `dir/`
# pattern does not match a symlink — git would stage it. A bare name matches
# both, so neither is ever tracked.

# Agent backends — provider config homes, session transcripts, machine ids.
# Never publish: delete the directory rather than rely on this one line.
.providers

# Rust build artefacts
rust/target
rust.bak

# Temporary scratch dirs
bak
tmp
.worktrees

# Derived analysis — regenerable from the C by `analyze`
codeql

# Machine-local: absolute paths to the crustify checkout, its skills and
# crustify-prim. Author one per machine from templates/cli-config.json.
cli-config.json

# Evaluation artefacts
stats.md
evaluation/

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TRACKED, deliberately — hand-authored or agent-produced, and NOT regenerable.
# Losing any of these loses work no rerun recovers:
#
#   crates.json                    the placement oracle (`.rs` home per entity)
#   build.json                     the build descriptor the extraction runs on
#   targets/*/scope-config.json    the authored port-scope definition
#   analysis/                      the manifests, carrying the agent-submitted
#                                  judgement fields (ownership / lifetime /
#                                  locking) that only a paid rerun reproduces
#
# The first three are read-only across a wave, so a worktree holding its own
# checked-out copy behaves identically. `analysis/` is WRITTEN mid-wave by
# `query --update`, so it is not symlinked either (see worktree._SHARED): each
# agent submits into its own copy and its landing commit carries the findings,
# the same route its Rust output already takes.
#
# Under targets/, only scope-config.json is authored — scope.json, deps-dag.json
# and logs/ are derived. Re-including one file from an excluded tree needs the
# descend-then-re-exclude ladder below, because git cannot re-include a file
# whose parent directory is excluded.
targets/*
!targets/*/
targets/*/*
!targets/*/scope-config.json
